//? Title: Signed reversals: exact small-permutation distance
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A signed gene order must be transformed into the positive identity by reversing
intervals and negating their strands. Finding the fewest reversals is a
shortest-path problem on signed permutations. We inspect actual BFS
predecessors, retain the original five-gene example, and distinguish breakpoint
bounds from exact distances.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Every inclusive reversal is its own inverse and has unit cost. BFS explores
states in nondecreasing reversal count; the first discovered identity therefore
has minimum distance. The path records are the actual stored first-discovery
predecessor chain.

Frame the permutation with0 and n+1 and count adjacent pairs (a,b) for which b
is not a+1. Reversing and negating an internal adjacent pair preserves whether
it is consecutive; only two boundary adjacencies change. Thus

$$ d\geq\lceil B/2\rceil.$$
\alttext(The exact reversal distance is at least the ceiling of half the framed breakpoint count.)

The old raw-breakpoint lower-bound wording was false: (-2,-1) has two
breakpoints but sorts in one reversal. There are 2^n times n factorial signed
permutations. This pedagogical exhaustive BFS is exponential and is not the
polynomial Hannenhalli-Pevzner method. Search limits throw without returning an
unproved optimum.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/signed_reversals_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::signed_reversal_trace({-2, -1});
    std::print("| path state | actual signed permutation | breakpoints | lower bound |\n| ---: | --- | ---: " "| ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.path.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &s = r.path[i];
        std::print("| {} | `{}` | {} | {} |\n", i, s.permutation, s.breakpoints,
                   (s.breakpoints + 1) / 2);
    }
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Signed order [-2, -1]");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} breakpoints, lower bound 1",
                                     r.path.front().breakpoints));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Exact distance {}", r.result.distance));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/signed_reversals_tiny.svg");
}
```
